# Vulnerable PHP Login Page (For Security Education)

This is the kind of code used in security training environments like DVWA. Understanding vulnerable code is essential for learning to prevent attacks.

## Database Setup (`db_setup.sql`)

```sql
CREATE DATABASE IF NOT EXISTS vuln_app;
USE vuln_app;

CREATE TABLE users (
    id INT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY,
    username VARCHAR(50) NOT NULL,
    password VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL
);

-- Sample credentials for testing
INSERT INTO users (username, password) VALUES
    ('admin', 'password123'),
    ('testuser', 'testpass456'),
    ('guest', 'guest789');
```

## Vulnerable Login Page (`login.php`)

```php
<?php
// VULNERABLE CODE - FOR EDUCATIONAL PURPOSES ONLY
// DO NOT USE IN PRODUCTION

$conn = mysqli_connect("localhost", "root", "", "vuln_app");

if ($_SERVER["REQUEST_METHOD"] == "POST") {
    $username = $_POST['username'];
    $password = $_POST['password'];

    // VULNERABILITY: User input is concatenated directly into the query
    // without any sanitization or parameterization
    $query = "SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = '$username' AND password = '$password'";

    $result = mysqli_query($conn, $query);

    if (mysqli_num_rows($result) > 0) {
        echo "<h2>Login successful!</h2>";
        echo "Welcome, " . htmlspecialchars($username);
    } else {
        echo "<h2>Login failed. Invalid credentials.</h2>";
    }
}
?>

<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Vulnerable Login</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Login</h1>
    <form method="POST" action="">
        <label>Username:</label>
        <input type="text" name="username" required><br><br>
        <label>Password:</label>
        <input type="password" name="password" required><br><br>
        <input type="submit" value="Login">
    </form>
</body>
</html>
```

## How the Injection Works

Entering this as the username (with any password) bypasses authentication:

```
' OR '1'='1' --
```

The resulting query becomes:

```sql
SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = '' OR '1'='1' --' AND password = '...'
```

Since `'1'='1'` is always true and `--` comments out the password check, all rows are returned.

## The Fix (Prepared Statements)

```php
// SECURE VERSION - Use prepared statements with parameter binding
$stmt = $conn->prepare("SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = ? AND password = ?");
$stmt->bind_param("ss", $username, $password);
$stmt->execute();
$result = $stmt->get_result();
```

**Key defenses:** prepared statements, parameterized queries, input validation, and storing passwords with `password_hash()`/`password_verify()` rather than plaintext (as shown above for simplicity).

